{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "An Emotional Week", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Let's Work It Out", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 6, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 4, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B2.1", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Describe ways of solving problems and getting things done in work and social contexts.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace. ", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": "Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace. ", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": "Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace. ", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": "Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace. ", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": "Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace. ", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": "Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace. ", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace. ", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace. ", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"content": "engenn9le04ob06re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "What Clauses and Long Noun Phrases as Subjects", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You shift emphasis from the subject of the sentence to another part of the sentence by using <span class=\"italic\">what</span>, <span class=\"italic\">that</span> and <span class=\"italic\">who</span> phrases as subjects.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Usually, you focus on the subject - that is the person or thing that is doing the action. <br/>  Example: <span class=\"italic\">Joan told a hilarious joke.</span><br/>(Joan does the action.)<br/><br/> When you want to emphasize a different part of the sentence, you isolate that part and put the rest of the information into a <span class=\"italic\">what</span>-phrase (or a <span class=\"italic\">that</span>-clause).<br/> Example: <span class=\"italic\">What was hilarious, was the joke Joan told.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You can use <span class=\"italic\">that</span> with a subordinate clause as the subject of a sentence.<br/> Example: <span class=\"italic\">That she imitated our teacher was funny.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">What</span> or <span class=\"italic\">Who</span> is placed at the beginning of a noun phrase. In this case, there is more emphasis on the content at the beginning of the sentence, so the person doing the action is not as important as other information, such as an adjective or verb in the sentence.<br/> Example:<br/>  <span class=\"italic\">What was hilarious was her imitation of the teacher.</span><br/>        <span class=\"italic\"> Who she should have made fun of was the principal.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "What was especially provocative was how it can be applied at work.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Who you really need to apply it to is your boss! ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "That I've been confused by her behavior isn't really surprising. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Who you should focus on changing a bit is you. ", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Tag Questions", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use tag questions to ask for clarification and confirmation.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": " A tag question is a short question at the end of  a statement. Tag questions are mostly used in speech when we want to:<ul><li><span class=\"strong\">confirm that something is true or is not true</span>, or</li> <li><span class=\"strong\">to get a reply</span> from someone we are speaking to.</li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "A <span class=\"strong\">positive</span> statement is followed by a <span class=\"strong\">negative</span> question tag.<br/> Use this when you think the other person will agree by saying 'yes'.<br/> <ul><li><span class=\"italic\">You <span class=\"strong\">were</span> taken by surprise, <span class=\"strong\">weren't</span> you?</li> <li><span class=\"italic\">You <span class=\"strong\">are</span> Canadian, <span class=\"strong\">aren't</span> you?</li> <li><span class=\"italic\">She <span class=\"strong\">has</span> a brother, <span class=\"strong\">doesn't</span> she?</li> <li><span class=\"italic\">He <span class=\"strong\">can</span> cook, <span class=\"strong\">can't</span> he?</span></li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "A negative statement is followed by a positive question tag.<br/> Use this when you think the other person will agree by saying 'no'.<br/> <ul><li><span class=\"italic\">You <span class=\"strong\">weren't</span> taken by surprise, <span class=\"strong\">were</span> you? </li> <li><span class=\"italic\">You <span class=\"strong\">aren't</span> Canadian, <span class=\"strong\">are</span> you?</li> <li><span class=\"italic\">She <span class=\"strong\">doesn't</span> have a brother, <span class=\"strong\">does</span> she?</li> <li><span class=\"italic\">He <span class=\"strong\">can't</span> cook, <span class=\"strong\">can</span> he?</li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "If the main verb is <span class=\"italic\">to be</span> or a <span class=\"strong\">modal</span> verb, the tag will be the same.<br/> For all other main verbs, use the tag <span class=\"italic\">do/does</span> or <span class=\"italic\">did/didn't</span>.<br/><br/> Look at the examples in the chart:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":12,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Main Verb\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Tag\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"He is/was\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"isn't/wasn't he?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"You are\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"aren't you?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"He can\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"can't he?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"We could\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"couldn't we?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"You will\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"won't you?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"She has/had\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"doesn't/didn't she?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"You have\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"don't you?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"He likes\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"doesn't he?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"You like\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"don't you?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"You liked\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"didn't you?\\\"}\"],\"styles\":